.TH APT\-SCOUT 1 "" "apt-scout 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.nh
.ad l
.ll 78n
.lt 78n
.SH NAME
apt\-scout \- find a fresh, fast Ubuntu archive mirror and point apt at it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B apt\-scout
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B apt\-scout
does what
.B nala fetch
does, without installing nala: it finds the official Ubuntu archive mirrors a
mirror list names, probes them, ranks them by freshness and then speed, and
either recommends one or rewrites the apt sources to use it. It is one static
binary, and the two packages it uses, \fBgpgv\fR and \fBubuntu\-keyring\fR,
are the ones the Debian package depends on. A system that lacks them still
ranks mirrors, but every signature then reads \fB?\fR and no write is
allowed.
.PP
A run does the following, in this order.
.IP 1. 4
It reads \fI/etc/os\-release\fR. \fBID\fR or \fBID_LIKE\fR must name Ubuntu or
a derivative such as Pop!_OS or Linux Mint, and the codename is
\fBUBUNTU_CODENAME\fR when present, otherwise \fBVERSION_CODENAME\fR. The
architecture comes from \fI/usr/bin/dpkg \-\-print\-architecture\fR: only
\fBamd64\fR and \fBi386\fR have a mirror choice, so any other architecture
stops the run with an explanation and changes nothing.
.IP 2. 4
Unless \fB\-\-no\-geo\fR is given, it detects the server's country and its
autonomous system (AS) from optional third-party sources. Every source is
optional: a failure is reported and the run continues, and if no country can be
found the run asks for \fB\-\-country\fR, or for \fB\-\-any\fR, which needs no
country at all. It never guesses a worldwide list.
.IP 3. 4
It loads the mirror list: Launchpad first, then
\fImirrors.ubuntu.com/<CC>.txt\fR for the country. The first valid answer wins
and the report names the source it used.
.IP 4. 4
Candidates are that one country's mirrors, unless \fB\-\-any\fR removes the
country from the set. They are deduplicated and, when the set is not narrowed
to one country, capped at 30 by the link speed Launchpad declares.
\fB\-\-https\-only\fR drops the plain http entries.
.IP 5. 4
Each candidate host is resolved and looked up in Team Cymru's DNS to learn its
AS. This is informational: a failed lookup leaves the AS unknown and is never a
reason to fail the run.
.IP 6. 4
It fetches the freshness reference once, from archive.ubuntu.com: the
\fI<codename>\-updates/InRelease\fR file in full with its OpenPGP signature
checked, and a 4 KiB head of \fI<codename>\-security/InRelease\fR, which is
never verified because nothing may consume its signature. If the reference host
is unreachable, the newest sane mirror date becomes the baseline and the run
warns. The \fI<codename>\fR release pocket is not compared, because its date is
the release date and never changes.
.IP 7. 4
It probes the candidates in parallel. The \fI\-updates\fR pocket's
\fBInRelease\fR file is downloaded in full so its signature can be checked;
latency is the time to first byte of that request, never the body download.
Throughput is measured on a 1 MiB range of a package index, because the
InRelease file is too small to measure it. At most three redirects are followed
and every hop is validated.
.IP 8. 4
It rejects unreachable mirrors, non-200 answers, and stale mirrors: one whose
\fI\-updates\fR date trails the reference by more than 24 hours. A missing or
unparsable date rejects too. The informational \fI\-security\fR lag never
rejects anything.
.IP 9. 4
It ranks what is left. Mirrors carrying the same \fI\-updates\fR date form
one group; within a group the highest throughput wins, then the lowest
latency,
and a mirror in the same AS as the server wins when its throughput is within
20% of the group's fastest. A lagging but not stale mirror stays selectable and
shows a warning.
.IP 10. 4
The uid decides what happens next; see \fBMODES\fR below.
.PP
A run narrowed to its own country that finds nothing usable there adds the
mirrors the same list names for every other country, probes them in one extra
pass, ranks them together with its own results and says why in one \fBnote:\fR
line. It never tops up a set that already has a usable mirror and never probes
a candidate twice. Widening reuses the list the run already loaded, so it needs
Launchpad: a run whose only list is the one-country \fImirrors.ubuntu.com\fR
file says it cannot widen and fails as it would have.
.PP
It rewrites only official Ubuntu archive URIs, and on Pop!_OS
\fIapt.pop-os.org/ubuntu\fR. \fIsecurity.ubuntu.com\fR, \fIports.ubuntu.com\fR,
PPAs and third-party repositories are never touched, and a line or stanza
carrying a \fI\-security\fR suite is left alone because \fI\-security\fR
freshness is not checked. The one exception is Pop!_OS, where the
\fI\-security\fR suite moves to a new file on the original mirror. After a
write, run \fBapt update\fR yourself; apt\-scout never runs it.
.SH OPTIONS
Every flag is an override, never a step in the workflow, and no flag is
required. A short form exists for every implemented flag except
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR. Short flags are never bundled, so \fB\-lyc NO\fR is an
unknown option.
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-country \fICC\fR
Use this country instead of the detected one. The value is uppercased and must
be two ASCII letters, and it must be one of the 82 countries Launchpad lists;
an unknown code is a usage error (exit 2) reported before any network request.
It changes the country only: the AS lookup still runs unless \fB\-\-no\-geo\fR
is given.
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-any
Ignore the detected and the given country and rank the mirrors the list names
for every country together. The country becomes display-only: it is still
detected or taken from \fB\-\-country\fR and still printed, and the header says
it constrains nothing. \fB\-\-any\fR with \fB\-\-country\fR is therefore not an
error, and \fB\-\-any\fR needs no country at all, so on its own it satisfies
the country requirement. It changes the candidate set only: the ranking and the
same-AS tie-break are unchanged, and it never widens.
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-no\-geo
Skip every geolocation and AS lookup, so ranking proceeds without AS
preference. It requires \fB\-\-country\fR or \fB\-\-any\fR, because nothing
else then says which country the run is in, and a run given neither is a usage
error (exit 2). It removes the lookups and not the mirror list: with
\fB\-\-country\fR the run still reads that country's mirrors, and with
\fB\-\-any\fR there is no \fImirrors.ubuntu.com/<CC>.txt\fR file to read, so
only Launchpad can supply the list.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-https\-only
Consider only https mirrors. It narrows the candidate set and nothing else: a
mirror an earlier run wrote over http stays recognizable, so it can still be
replaced.
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-top \fIN\fR
Show \fIN\fR rows in the ranked table. The default is 5, and \fIN\fR must be a
positive integer. It is display only: it changes how many rows are printed, not
which candidates are probed, ranked, recommended or written, and not the exit
code.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
Add the proof behind the default report; \fBOUTPUT\fR states exactly what is
added. It is display only: two runs that differ only in this flag probe, rank,
plan and write identically.
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-list
Print the ranked table and the change a write would make, and write nothing,
whatever the uid. A run that stops because no apt source may be rewritten, or
because a write would be refused, reports that and still exits 0. It changes
the mode and not the discovery: the same candidates are probed and ranked.
.TP
.B \-y, \-\-yes
Skip the one confirmation before a write in a root run. It removes the prompt
and nothing else: every validation still runs before the first byte, so no
answer to the prompt could have bypassed it anyway. It has no effect in a
non-root run or with \fB\-\-list\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Print no colour whatever the terminal is. It is the one implemented flag
without a short form. It is display only: the plain report carries the same
words, the same columns and the same headings, minus the escape sequences.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print the help text and exit 0 before any run starts.
.PP
\fB\-r\fR and \fB\-R\fR are reserved for \fB\-\-restore\fR and
\fB\-\-refresh\fR and are not accepted yet, and neither are \fB\-\-restore\fR,
\fB\-\-refresh\fR and \fB\-\-tui\fR themselves. A run given any of them fails
as an unknown option with exit 2; see \fBLIMITATIONS\fR.
.SH MODES
Discovery, ranking and the recommendation are the same in every mode; the uid,
not a flag, decides whether the run writes.
.TP
.B apt\-scout
As a non-root user: no write. The run prints the report and then one line,
\fBsudo apt\-scout ...\fR, rebuilt from the flags of that run and spelled in
long form so a copied command is unambiguous, and exits 3. It opens no apt file
for writing.
.TP
.B sudo apt\-scout
As root: write. After the report, the planned change names the files it will
change, the old URI, the new one and the backup path it will create, and one
confirmation asks to apply it; \fB\-\-yes\fR skips that prompt. The files
are backed up first, then each target is replaced atomically.
.TP
.B apt\-scout \-\-list
Any uid: no write, exit 0. The ranked table and the planned change are printed;
nothing under \fI/etc/apt\fR is opened for writing and no backup is created.
.PP
A candidate set that is not narrowed to one country is capped at 30 mirrors,
chosen by the link speed Launchpad declares: the page names about 560 mirrors,
and probing them all is not a burst any run makes. The cap decides what is
probed and never what ranks, so a mirror whose declared speed is unknown sorts
last among the candidates and still ranks wherever its probe puts it. A run
narrowed to one country keeps the cap in list order.
.PP
The one automatic widening of \fBDESCRIPTION\fR happens only when every
candidate of the run's own country was rejected. It is never a preference and
never a top-up, and it needs Launchpad, which is the only list that names every
country. \fB\-\-any\fR never widens: its set is already every country, so an
empty one means nothing usable anywhere.
.SH OUTPUT
The report is one block on stdout, printed once probing and ranking are done,
so nothing printed while the run works can split it. Its sections, in this
order:
.TP
.B identity line
.br
The program version, the detected distribution and the country, with the origin
of that country.
.TP
.B Sources
.br
The read-only inventory of \fI/etc/apt\fR, giving each file inspected and each
entry skipped with its reason.
.TP
.B context line
.br
\fBmirrors:\fR naming the mirror list the run used, and \fBreference:\fR naming
the freshness baseline and its signature status.
.TP
.B Ranked
.br
The table, titled with how many mirrors are usable and how many were rejected,
above a one-clause legend for each column that needs one.
.TP
.B Detail
.br
Printed only by \fB\-\-verbose\fR: one block per row with the facts the table
compresses.
.TP
.B Recommendation
.br
The chosen mirror, its own country (which differs from the run's once the run
is not narrowed to one country), how far behind it is, its speed, its latency
and its signature status, with the reason it was chosen.
.TP
.B Planned change
.br
The files a write would change, the old and the new URI, and the backup path;
\fB\-\-list\fR says that nothing is written.
.TP
.B Rejected
.br
Printed only when something was rejected: the reasons grouped by class, with a
count each, or the full text of every reason under \fB\-\-verbose\fR.
.PP
A section with nothing to say prints nothing at all: no heading and no zero
count.
.PP
Without \fB\-v\fR the report answers which mirror to use and why: the identity
line, \fBSources\fR, the context line without the server's AS, the table, the
recommendation, the planned change with the backup path, and the rejection
counts grouped by class.
.PP
With \fB\-v\fR it also answers prove it to me, adding each row's AS number and
its \fBsame\fR/\fBdiff\fR/\fBunknown\fR match with the server, the server's AS
on the context line, each row's country and the address the run resolved, both
pocket dates in full, the \fI\-security\fR lag, the exact signature status, an
uncut redirect host, and the full text of every rejection reason.
.PP
The table has a rank, the mirror URL, and these columns:
.TP
.B behind
.br
How far the mirror's \fI<codename>\-updates\fR package lists trail the freshest
date the run knows, in minutes, \fB0m\fR when the mirror is up to date. It is
freshness, never network latency, and it is the same number the rejection rule
compares against.
.TP
.B speed
.br
The measured throughput in MB/s.
.TP
.B latency
The measured round trip of the mirror's \fI\-updates\fR request, in
milliseconds. It is network time, never freshness.
.TP
.B verified
The signature of the \fI<codename>\-updates\fR \fBInRelease\fR file: \fByes\fR
when \fBgpgv\fR accepted it with the Ubuntu archive keyring, \fBno\fR when the
body was readable but unsigned, altered or signed by an unknown key, and
\fB?\fR when verification could not run at all \(em a missing \fBgpgv\fR or
keyring, an oversized body, or a timeout. Only \fByes\fR may authorize a write;
\fBno\fR and \fB?\fR never do, and neither is a reason to reject or reorder a
row.
.TP
.B notes
.br
\fBredir:<host>\fR when a mirror redirects to another host; a host that does
not fit is cut and ends in \fB~\fR. The column is printed only when some shown
row carries a note, and \fB\-\-verbose\fR prints the host uncut.
.PP
A \fB\-\-verbose\fR run adds one more column, the AS number, and one line under
the table when no AS data could be had at all.
.PP
While it works, a run shows one transient status line on stderr, rewritten in
place: one Braille cell advanced about every 100 ms, naming the phase it is in
(loading the mirror list, verifying the reference, probing with \fBn/m\fR, or
ranking), and ending with one summary line:
.RS 4
\fBdone \(em <usable> usable, <rejected> rejected in <t> s\fR
.RE
.PP
That line appears only when stderr is a terminal. When stderr is a pipe, a file
or a CI log, nothing at all is written and nothing is started to draw one. A
warning keeps its own line, and the animation stops while it is on screen.
.PP
Colour appears only when stdout is a terminal, \fBNO_COLOR\fR is not set and
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR was not given. Headings and the identity line are bold, the
\fByes\fR cell is green while \fBno\fR and \fB?\fR are yellow, a rejection
reason and a line removed from a source file are red while an added line is
green, and the numeric column headings are dim. Nothing else is styled, and the
\fBsudo apt\-scout\fR line is never styled because a user copies it.
.PP
A write prints a second block after the confirmation: \fBbackup: <path>\fR, one
\fBwrote <path>\fR per file, and a closing line naming the files and suggesting
\fBapt update\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including a \fB\-\-list\fR run that would write, and a root run whose
sources already point at the chosen mirror.
.TP
.B 1
A refusal or a failure after the run started: no usable or no fresh mirror; no
apt source that may be rewritten; a backup or a write that failed; a plan
refused by validation. A root run that found nothing to write exits 1.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, reported before any network request: an unknown option, including
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-R\fR, \fB\-\-tui\fR, \fB\-\-restore\fR and \fB\-\-refresh\fR;
\fB\-\-no\-geo\fR without \fB\-\-country\fR or \fB\-\-any\fR; an unknown
country code; a value the flag cannot use; or a country that could not be
detected and was not given.
.TP
.B 3
The run would have written and this uid may not: every non-root run other than
\fB\-\-list\fR that reaches the write. The line to run instead is printed.
.TP
.B 4
Unsupported distribution or architecture: a system that is not Ubuntu or one of
its derivatives; an architecture other than \fBamd64\fR or \fBi386\fR, because
\fIports.ubuntu.com\fR has no mirror choice; or a codename that
archive.ubuntu.com answers 404 for, because the release moved to
\fIold\-releases.ubuntu.com\fR, which has no mirrors.
.SH FILES
\fI/etc/apt/sources.list\fR, \fI/etc/apt/sources.list.d/*.list\fR and
\fI/etc/apt/sources.list.d/*.sources\fR are read for the source inventory and
are the files a rewrite targets. Symlinks are refused, every other field and
comment is preserved byte for byte, and a stanza that is disabled is left
alone.
.TP
.I /var/backups/apt-scout/<id>/
Created by a write before its first byte, mode 0700. \fI<id>\fR is the UTC
instant of the run in the form \fBYYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ\fR. The directory holds a
copy of every file the write replaces and the \fBMANIFEST\fR below.
.TP
.I /var/backups/apt-scout/<id>/MANIFEST
Mode 0600. The record of that backup: a header line, the id, the file count,
one line per file giving the target, the action, the SHA-256 of the bytes, the
mode, the uid and the gid, and a closing \fBend <n>\fR line. It is the record
that a restore is defined against, and the only file that makes a directory a
backup.
.TP
.I /usr/share/keyrings/ubuntu-archive-keyring.gpg
The keyring every signature check uses, shipped by the \fBubuntu\-keyring\fR
package.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/gpgv
The verifier, named by its fixed path and never resolved through \fBPATH\fR,
shipped by the \fBgpgv\fR package.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Any value, the empty one included, turns colour off. It is a display-only read:
it reaches no decision, no path, no byte written and no exit code.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
.br
Selects where the verifier creates its private directory, which is also the
\fBgpgv\fR home directory. It reaches no \fI/etc/apt\fR path and no byte
written; a value that names no usable directory makes verification unavailable,
and a write then fails closed.
.TP
.B LC_ALL, LANG
Planned, not read today: no message catalog ships yet, so every message is
English whatever these say. When they are read they will choose printed words
only, and they will reach no decision, no path, a byte written or an exit code.
.SH SECURITY
Every candidate's \fI<codename>\-updates\fR \fBInRelease\fR is verified with
\fB/usr/bin/gpgv\fR and the Ubuntu archive keyring before a write may use it,
and only a \fByes\fR authorizes a write.
.PP
The plan a write executes is validated against the on-disk bytes it was
computed from. A target that is not a regular file after resolution, a mismatch
with the file as it is on disk, a digest the writer cannot recompute, or any
field it cannot check aborts the write before its first byte.
.PP
The backup comes first: \fI/var/backups/apt-scout/<id>/\fR is created mode 0700
with a 0600 \fBMANIFEST\fR, and a backup that fails aborts the whole write with
nothing written.
.PP
Each target is replaced atomically: a temporary file in the same directory, an
fsync, and a rename. Its mode and its owner are kept.
.PP
A non-root run cannot write: the writer refuses, and the run prints the
\fBsudo\fR line and exits 3.
.PP
The write path runs no shell, resolves no command through \fBPATH\fR, makes no
network request of its own, and reads no environment variable. The program
never re-executes itself privileged, never invokes \fBsudo\fR and is never
installed setuid: privilege comes only from the user running it as root.
.PP
The confirmation prompt is a safety rail, not a security control. \fB\-\-yes\fR
removes only the prompt; the guarantee is the validation above.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.RS 4
apt\-scout \-l              # preview: table and planned change
sudo apt\-scout             # root: confirm, then write
sudo apt\-scout \-y         # root: write without the prompt
sudo apt\-scout \-c DE      # switch to a mirror in Germany instead
apt\-scout \-a \-n          # every country, no geolocation lookups
sudo apt\-scout \-v \-t 10  # the proof behind the report, 10 rows
.RE
.fi
.SH LIMITATIONS
\fB\-\-restore\fR, \fB\-\-tui\fR and \fB\-\-refresh\fR are not implemented yet:
a run given one of them, or given the reserved \fB\-r\fR or \fB\-R\fR, fails as
an unknown option with exit 2. This page documents only what ships, which is
why they are absent from \fBOPTIONS\fR and from \fBMODES\fR. Localized pages
are planned for the locales that ship a message catalog; this English page is
the source of truth until then.
